# For AI assistants (not for human sysops)

This file is Cursor/agent guidance. Do not copy it into sysop-facing docs
(`docs/sysop-guide.md`, `docs/themes.md`, `docs/screens.md`, plugin READMEs).

## Two audiences, one contract

Sysops either edit the board themselves or **direct you** to edit it.
The second path is first-class. Prefer the same drop-a-file contracts a
human would use (`themes/*.theme`, `plugins/<name>/screens/`, `screens/`).
Do not invent a Python palette, a new pipeline, or an in-board editor
when a file will do.

First start should feel like a working BBS (TriBBS-simple), not a tour of
every knob. Change the one file they asked for. Do not dump architecture
or a 12-step config unless they asked.

Call things what they are. A new directory or file should be obvious from
its name (`server/`, `themes/`, `run_server.py`) — not "gateway node".
If a sysop cannot start a basic board from `README.md`, the on-ramp is
wrong; do not send them to architecture first.

## Launching the server

When you start or restart `run_server.py`, tell the user the **version
and build** from `core.version.display()` (for example `0.2.0 (8eae965)`),
plus the ports. Same line as `/ver`.

After the listener is up:

```bash
venv/bin/python -c "from core.version import display; print(display())"
```

Bind on the host (not the Cursor sandbox) or SyncTERM will not see
127.0.0.1.

Stop or restart the board with the soft API first (connected users get
a goodbye):

```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/shutdown
```

Optional JSON body: `{"message": "..."}`. Do not use `pkill`. `kill
-TERM` on the python PID that owns 6400/6422 is last resort only — if
the API is down or the process ignores shutdown. Never `-9`.
